  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "A cyclist accelerates with constant acceleration from $0\\,\\text{m/s}$ to $20\\,\\text{m/s}$ in $10$ seconds. Find their acceleration.",
  "worked_solution": "We can find the acceleration of an object by computing the slope of its speed-time graph.  To plot a speed-time graph, we need two points.\n\n- The cyclist starts at a speed of $0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ This corresponds to the point $(0,0).$\n\n- In $10$ seconds, the cyclist reaches a speed of $20\\,\\text{m/s}.$ This corresponds to the point $(10,20).$\n\nSince the acceleration is constant, we plot the two points and draw a line through them as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/e60d9d6898bd8d49fb32860c439cb022c25850ff609b80ee486e71b48203b5fc.png)\n\nThe slope of the cyclist's speed-time graph gives its acceleration. The slope of this line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{20\\text{\u00a0m/s}-0\\text{\u00a0m/s}}{10\\text{\u00a0s}-0\\text{\u00a0s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{20\\text{\u00a0m/s}}{10\\text{\u00a0s}} \\\\  & =2\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cyclist's acceleration is $2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$",
